#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556172 :question-generator/id #uuid "1517b8a7-b8eb-5039-9dec-9ce81c5652e6" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186124439.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ec35ef-bc6e-5ae3-be18-9b9ce4679dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321669} {:answer/id #uuid "8bdb8a5e-3b0d-5134-98cf-e35ce5f13f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322265} {:answer/id #uuid "a0be3297-c3f7-5b45-9e19-ac84539d01ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322415} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dab9bc-d4e9-5357-b42b-704f2f3b619c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322559} {:answer/id #uuid "feac73a2-6cf2-5fa6-aa53-e2dafe29b2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feac73a2-6cf2-5fa6-aa53-e2dafe29b2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323157} :answer-field/id #uuid "307ef176-8c07-5616-a330-828891c316a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a2ac51-d5be-54ed-b565-78bd1a3a67e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36371" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-3 \\\\ z &=6x-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407304} {:answer/id #uuid "5de2add0-6423-5754-a07f-96730ef51cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407307} {:answer/id #uuid "bccf173f-8570-55d3-b189-673a90a1c377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407310} {:answer/id #uuid "db135884-f54c-58f1-b6d6-52b5e9137f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407312} {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ef3e-d356-53fe-b14e-c4931ae0f4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407304} :answer-field/id #uuid "a315569d-b4ce-597c-a11e-e51ae4013a54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecca0679-247f-506b-95a3-b2be27b80569" :question/math-academy-id "q-64972" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1843e8dd-af0c-51f7-a63f-d47f4be0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae2b17b-4d19-5b24-bc0c-59e77736ad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267387} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b280b-d5f7-568a-8d81-2dd2a22c34a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267583} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0faa48-7712-5417-80bf-a7c921e7985b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268366} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc676ee-4730-534b-a0c7-fe2034b61a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1843e8dd-af0c-51f7-a63f-d47f4be0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba3e928-a7c0-5873-a82b-0e92f1edc942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7da12630-1fa6-595d-a465-5095a1603e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132599" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd176d1-71e6-5d2d-a259-a3fcc0af991f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266962} {:answer/id #uuid "62df8b67-d01e-577d-9807-c0b20a784a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267013} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8b3ad7-9956-5469-b111-e37bf7bc1bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267549} {:answer/id #uuid "b0142210-122c-5cb4-9c32-769b018c4a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729} {:answer/id #uuid "dcebd8e5-4a98-5a88-b29f-0e1f28a253c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0142210-122c-5cb4-9c32-769b018c4a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c4369a-8e31-5a0a-8b79-02cda6e1bfbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86de2721-fbfb-53fc-8404-fca3429d9ecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132601" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b4af52-0cb5-5170-b305-ddbac150686d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267149} {:answer/id #uuid "a7393b59-39d3-5aad-8cbc-5f6ab101035b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267660} {:answer/id #uuid "bc72c95f-ce36-52f6-88e0-ea6a281e11ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267831} {:answer/id #uuid "c452d620-354c-5b6f-b5ca-b7e2e4c22b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267897} {:answer/id #uuid "dfecef19-f61e-5834-b586-b5f0a09dbb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfecef19-f61e-5834-b586-b5f0a09dbb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268121} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75921e8-74b4-5a5e-9fcb-280d1ebead77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecd11e65-1ebe-5f6c-b826-3c8a8ba99741" :question/math-academy-id "q-132602" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a781070-386b-5885-ae90-e1131fceb047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266514} {:answer/id #uuid "36c5d600-802b-585f-b814-467c86175273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266615} {:answer/id #uuid "897a6535-e50c-5904-94d0-9f0a5507508b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267366} {:answer/id #uuid "e1546c65-4c85-51ab-b887-32752413d5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268132} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb2a519-673a-5b66-a103-b6c0fb7b7e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2j-3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a781070-386b-5885-ae90-e1131fceb047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266514} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf130c40-9ba8-59b2-9288-24b063913d80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e644a49d-2ffc-52d7-91ba-f94a7b4d9023" :question/math-academy-id "q-132604" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d882919-8d13-50f7-840d-8a1f45fcd980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333253} {:answer/id #uuid "543ab52d-1623-54f6-b166-c0e43d208801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333756} {:answer/id #uuid "a803a6f4-b31b-5b47-92f1-472e09aa720a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334459} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b4881-8db8-5a4e-a73c-944bf83e8664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335177} {:answer/id #uuid "fa97ca9a-10f2-55a2-b950-5d1537242daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b4881-8db8-5a4e-a73c-944bf83e8664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335177} :answer-field/id #uuid "234510dd-0314-5bd3-949a-5dd7a7a7bea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "725a1fb6-c62e-5bb7-9fe8-49c03c5250ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-64971" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336053} {:answer/id #uuid "208d69f2-61c9-5cef-ae67-df4b82077458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336339} {:answer/id #uuid "4733129a-0c72-56e1-9873-d6951efbe108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336692} {:answer/id #uuid "54939340-4ca9-5a82-b24c-2542ed64af8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336814} {:answer/id #uuid "fb04c736-1e93-5392-937a-e6b52b89f395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336053} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee8ed97-cdf9-56b8-9f86-2a9258c8b74b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d6bbe53-bb73-5d24-b77f-7c5fef3fe5bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71220" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-y-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a4a9dc-c717-5035-ba3e-987961f9ee72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336198} {:answer/id #uuid "3f159693-7272-539b-8d07-778a3ed4938c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336624} {:answer/id #uuid "596e8a9c-9c2e-5135-a08e-ad772219a349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336849} {:answer/id #uuid "749562ba-1b02-5f83-a973-8bffa1c80cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337081} {:answer/id #uuid "de8038e5-bdf2-5d72-8003-0ca0304fe0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596e8a9c-9c2e-5135-a08e-ad772219a349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336849} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6508aa-26cc-5e67-a21e-e797c69eabff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b7dd45b-b8d1-582b-9fbf-c50179d63d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71221" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a0d643-3fba-5025-8ba0-fe80f13a0f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336191} {:answer/id #uuid "1a106852-4afd-52ed-ab68-75af9121d300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336273} {:answer/id #uuid "7079191a-2817-517c-b22b-ed0b7decbd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337033} {:answer/id #uuid "903c10f0-0ae9-5406-ba48-0978c42fa7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337339} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b520c6-d634-55fb-9a73-9aac376cbf2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a0d643-3fba-5025-8ba0-fe80f13a0f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336191} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc44ecd5-8b94-53f1-aa64-9fdcc88bcaac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dcde73b-e9a7-56a8-9973-7e22f495be27" :question/math-academy-id "q-71234" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x(y-x)}{2x(y+1)}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ed7ce5-304c-5eac-b1cb-5f8c4b4d23e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383316} {:answer/id #uuid "4d34dabe-6639-51a1-abc1-40a551267c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383317} {:answer/id #uuid "7f96ce17-18b2-5a3f-8e28-936aabc05996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383318} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fe8dda-e437-53fb-9f60-09b8a339e900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383319} {:answer/id #uuid "650c5858-626f-56bb-bae0-7ffa49b7eab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d34dabe-6639-51a1-abc1-40a551267c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383317} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a3324e-1ddd-5ca6-b734-63094c92f926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d71daf7a-6da8-5e30-8340-64d86d4d3ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-132609" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & ={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)(y-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(y-1) \\\\ z & =2y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2y-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-2$ (or $2y-z-2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2187dab-e7ba-58a2-bb51-8cf8f2621861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385496} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b20770-5530-508f-960d-cfa8e2d86fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385497} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c3159c-2549-5a94-a72e-e70fd7aa61b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385498} {:answer/id #uuid "45723610-f576-57d8-968d-eb78eab9df33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385499} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cd429b-6112-5d6c-a161-6002e343448a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1c3159c-2549-5a94-a72e-e70fd7aa61b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385498} :answer-field/id #uuid "472f77ac-e8ea-5a7a-80e6-2f7ea6d432cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "851f656d-69a9-5c8d-8482-1385f4b7fe8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71224" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{2}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & ={f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)(y-0) \\\\ z-2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ (or $y+2z-4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-2,1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406792} {:answer/id #uuid "43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406793} {:answer/id #uuid "60dae67c-4a44-58da-b15e-79beacb9c712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406794} {:answer/id #uuid "652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406795} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406793} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-71231" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7b7abb-15fc-5e25-af71-ce99a58976f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425091} {:answer/id #uuid "14e7d503-39ad-5577-a641-1da1d8c70d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425094} {:answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425098} {:answer/id #uuid "69c34c73-ac38-5363-8c2d-999f5d258274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425103} {:answer/id #uuid "759cdd15-f659-5676-8d91-9b242c2d2b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425098} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-71228" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)-(x+y)^{3}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)-(1+0)^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =-1(y) \\\\ z+1 & =-y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425092} {:answer/id #uuid "1291615e-eaf6-5649-a612-304df178a8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425093} {:answer/id #uuid "23478b53-a88f-568a-858a-7b25f93a7066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425095} {:answer/id #uuid "7733cd29-624f-51f2-ae97-a884afdbb38d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425105} {:answer/id #uuid "e4854410-0525-5e63-82a6-354e8d687ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425092} :answer-field/id #uuid "472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71226" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=5.$ Given that the point $P(4,5,-2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(4,5).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,5) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}{2(4)-(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(4,5) & ={f}_{x}(4,5)(x-4) \\\\ z-(-2) & =1(x-4) \\\\ z+2 & =x-4 \\\\ z & =x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,5,-2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5 \\\\ z &=x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=5,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-6$ (or $x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fcaa4c-cdc7-55cc-9fbc-bf020baece7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556104} {:answer/id #uuid "3aaf0daf-4db1-56a4-85c0-b9d13bb393d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556117} {:answer/id #uuid "89d7a47f-5ffb-5fb7-8773-673d0bdca988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556142} {:answer/id #uuid "f703c35e-0bb7-5ae6-ac28-2ef517196952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556167} {:answer/id #uuid "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556169} :answer-field/id #uuid "778574b8-fa99-5187-b7ff-a835f64fafed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "055263bb-00e6-5ecd-9052-3f0991382dda" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(-2)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =(-2)(-2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =4 x + 8 \\\\ z & =4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4 x + 8$ (or $4 x - z + 8=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 4 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-4⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-4⟩)=⟨1,0,4⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3e1e95-6abb-5b2d-b333-2c9928f10fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556107} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3dd612-7efb-572c-98e3-50b684d7a1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556126} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ce72c-9b00-57d7-ab95-a6ee0fc09ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556138} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5fd2fc-a327-5afc-af0f-ecca1c720272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556150} {:answer/id #uuid "b298ee27-0c63-5917-9d3e-0528f700c1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ce72c-9b00-57d7-ab95-a6ee0fc09ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556138} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f426c50-eced-5e9c-a349-73aa145929b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24dbd77f-2590-58ef-821c-6bd1e8a62bdc" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(3)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =3 x + 6 \\\\ z & =3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 x + 2$ (or $3 x - z + 2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 3 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-3⟩)=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015bc2bf-13ce-5c4f-8f0c-a4a74231f0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556102} {:answer/id #uuid "58d0c333-0126-5eb6-89df-cfb21a2d9ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-6,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556124} {:answer/id #uuid "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556146} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc46a02-9995-525c-8cb6-ea5098fa7457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556157} {:answer/id #uuid "ff84763e-3842-594a-98b1-79f342d9e445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556146} :answer-field/id #uuid "646eda7e-2147-525c-aef1-fd3fda68bebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "322047e2-3976-5636-bcd1-e92ee6b570c4" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(-2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =(-2)(3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =18 - 6 y \\\\ z & =15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=15 - 6 y$ (or $- 6 y - z + 15=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-6⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556108} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3b10f7-1ab2-5dd0-bdc2-2c023a53bdc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556121} {:answer/id #uuid "65a60baf-69b2-5fe6-90a1-c8f938f6de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556130} {:answer/id #uuid "72d61d09-f7e2-5f33-b750-f198d1889040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556131} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e01698-f591-5f18-9466-8532bda73ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556108} :answer-field/id #uuid "777c8854-9f2f-5bcb-b4b6-3afe85450d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48d0933d-38f4-5fca-ac17-2cb0bd7b696b" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =6 - 2 y \\\\ z & =3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 - 2 y$ (or $- 2 y - z + 3=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37dd67a6-9302-52ce-a4eb-b458c3c38495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556115} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4acddb-f393-5ef8-8512-f2734c2b7b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,8,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556136} {:answer/id #uuid "87120362-d259-56d3-979a-9bd2346dda46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,8,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556139} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c1d413-8151-5d44-94fe-2a943194fc87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556151} {:answer/id #uuid "c164c3ce-a97a-5789-ae86-81689ef5e741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c164c3ce-a97a-5789-ae86-81689ef5e741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556153} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4340bac-2429-5c45-a387-f044a1812b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76fc75e5-bc34-5a11-827a-718027bfa6d6" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=8$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(8)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =8 y - 16 \\\\ z & =8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=8 y - 19$ (or $8 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,8,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,8,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 8 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,8⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49af77ba-23b1-5c02-ae78-e0a7f3dcabf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556119} {:answer/id #uuid "5a03e80c-0292-50fe-a3ba-354f93996f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556125} {:answer/id #uuid "63614bc9-8e20-5bf1-8110-d978ebea603a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556128} {:answer/id #uuid "dfefda43-a9c6-542a-aa55-6d293ada18bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556161} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0f23c0-bab4-5a6d-b985-d5db0527d0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfefda43-a9c6-542a-aa55-6d293ada18bf" :answer/type 