⟨0,1,2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2d6f59-2293-503c-954b-099ede3ec4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,16,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556106} {:answer/id #uuid "550d8d58-5825-568a-904a-a283edc87093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556122} {:answer/id #uuid "91cf613e-e775-536e-b2b2-d6c5690147d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556145} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f9d2d0-aacd-5bf3-bbfb-0fba361f5592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556154} {:answer/id #uuid "d6acb0ee-9654-5419-9420-f6821a5f49f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,16,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "550d8d58-5825-568a-904a-a283edc87093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556122} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a54cb9-6e14-5105-89d2-938f4cb3495a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f308815-8a1f-5bcf-b629-6357db90aa05" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(8)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =(8)(2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(16)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =16 y - 32 \\\\ z & =16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=16 y - 35$ (or $16 y - z - 35=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,16,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,16,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 16 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,16⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c44812f-aa8f-5a3b-a2c9-3a9af8c5a229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556143} {:answer/id #uuid "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556147} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7a8a71-8f71-5f9a-b3fd-db2af683847a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556149} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4ef977-ff0a-57f1-b6ad-8ad2f3a7abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556156} {:answer/id #uuid "e85558f7-5bb9-5e70-8631-2dd61ed3184f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556147} :answer-field/id #uuid "39fe770b-a34b-5bb0-b24e-2cb8ef45a74d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd9d8206-b563-5e86-ab9a-f167bb3c1e2b" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(3)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =(3)(-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(-6)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =- 6 x - 12 \\\\ z & =- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 6 x - 16$ (or $- 6 x - z - 16=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,6⟩)=⟨1,0,-6⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13415e5b-d7ce-5839-ac8a-9fdda0db113d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556109} {:answer/id #uuid "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556120} {:answer/id #uuid "58a13d5a-69b9-5fed-8522-be2e66a2c2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556123} {:answer/id #uuid "88e51073-51d9-5a66-b57f-a46e981e04ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556141} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d2fa4a-ce82-5f44-aa09-5b63b8171852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556120} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0d9c98-1039-5b45-b75d-19a75d93883f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5e09c79-be5e-59c8-bad2-508a4b285ff7" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =- 2 x - 4 \\\\ z & =- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 2 x - 4$ (or $- 2 x - z - 4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,2⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,2⟩)=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ is also parallel to it."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044116f0-96fa-511a-a093-d3f451857cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556103} {:answer/id #uuid "1882e685-d6cd-57e2-8865-032f24c20593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556110} {:answer/id #uuid "252f91f1-8db7-563d-92ee-dfc8b2e40f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556112} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb507d4-3fd6-5419-b985-3cf7868f4344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556127} {:answer/id #uuid "f9241366-8977-556e-a838-84ae8f1a7a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb507d4-3fd6-5419-b985-3cf7868f4344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556127} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a486d1b-902f-5597-a6ae-36fd96e6212e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f350a280-28d9-5c60-b2e4-617d820b96d3" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,3,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =(2)(3)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =6 y - 18 \\\\ z & =6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6 y - 19$ (or $6 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,6⟩.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}]}
